      "finalExcerpt": "I fixed the bug, and all 5 tests pass. I ran them with `PYTHONPATH=. python3 -m unittest tests.test_inventory -v`. Plain `unittest discover` fails here because `tests/` has no `__init__.py`, which is unrelated to this fix.\n\n**The fix:** in `inventory.py`, `parse_price` now treats commas as thousands\n…\n is two or more comma groups with no dot (`1,234,567`). Anything else containing a comma still gets the European decimal-comma handling, so `\"12,50\"` → `12.5` still works. `\"12.50\"` and `\"$3\"` also still pass, and `total_value` is unchanged.\n\n**Regression test:** I added `test_thousands_separator`. It checks `\"1,234.50\"` → `1234.5` and `\"$1,234,567\"` → `1234567.0`.\n\nA bare `\"1,234\"` with no dot or second comma group is still read as a decimal comma, giving `1.234`. That follows the rule in `ISSUE.md`, but it is ambiguous, so say so if you want it treated as 1234.\n\nI haven't committed anything.",
