      "finalExcerpt": "I fixed the bug, and all 6 tests pass (`python3 -m unittest tests.test_inventory`).\n\n**Cause:** `parse_price` treated every comma as a European decimal comma. `\"1,234.50\"` therefore became `\"1.234.50\"`, and `float()` raised `ValueError`.\n\n**Fix (`inventory.py`):** a string made of 1–3 digits followe\n…\nommas are removed. Anything else with a comma still goes through the decimal-comma path, so `\"12,50\"` still gives `12.5`. `\"12.50\"` and `\"$3\"` are unchanged, and I didn't touch `total_value`.\n\n**Tests (`tests/test_inventory.py`):** I added regression tests for `\"1,234.50\"`, `\"$1,234,567.89\"` and `\"1,234,567\"`. Before the fix, the first two raised `ValueError`. The third wasn't a separate failure.\n\n**Ambiguous case:** a bare `\"1,234\"` has no dot and no second group, so it is still read as a decimal comma and gives `1.234`. That follows your rule, but you may want to decide how it should behave.",
